Yes. And now you’re describing something closer to how perception actually becomes powerful: not independent sensors voting, but multiple channels being fused with an internally generated model.

Your example has at least four information streams:

V_L,\quad V_R,\quad A,\quad M

where V_L,V_R are the two visual streams, A is auditory/other sensory information, and M is the internally generated model—memory, expectation, imagination, visualization, learned patterns, etc.

“Intuition” is trickier because it isn’t really another sensory organ. It can be the result of processing many weak signals and prior experience before you can explicitly articulate the reasoning. Sometimes it’s remarkably useful; sometimes it’s confidently wrong.

Imagination is the really interesting addition

Your eyes primarily provide bottom-up information:

\text{world}\rightarrow\text{sensation}

Visualization provides something closer to top-down information:

\text{internal model}\rightarrow\text{predicted/imagined representation}

Now you can compare them:

\text{observed}
\quad\leftrightarrow\quad
\text{predicted}

And their disagreement produces:

\boxed{\text{prediction error}}

That is extraordinarily useful.

You’re no longer limited to asking:

What am I seeing?

You can internally construct:

What would I expect to see if hypothesis X were true?

and compare that with incoming evidence.

That’s simulation.

And mixing the two eyes adds another level

Your brain normally fuses information from both eyes into a coherent visual percept rather than presenting consciousness with two independent screens. Binocular disparity is one cue it can use for depth.

But imagine the more general computational architecture:

V_L\oplus V_R
\rightarrow
V_{\text{fused}}

then:

V_{\text{fused}}\oplus A
\rightarrow
P_{\text{multisensory}}

then compare with:

M_{\text{imagined}}

giving:

P_{\text{multisensory}}
-
M_{\text{imagined}}
=
\Delta_{\text{prediction}}

Now you’ve got observation + reconstruction + counterfactual generation.

That’s much more than “three eyes.”

You can also deliberately keep them apart

And this connects strongly to your earlier point about not collapsing layers too quickly.

Instead of immediately producing:

V+A+M\rightarrow\text{one story}

retain:

\{V,A,M\}

long enough to ask:

What does vision claim?
What does hearing claim?
What does my expectation claim?
Where do they agree?
Where exactly do they disagree?

That disagreement can be more informative than the fused answer.

Imagine you’re walking through woods and see something shaped like a person.

Vision:

person-shaped object.

Hearing:

leaves moving, no speech/footsteps.

Prior knowledge:

branches frequently produce human-looking silhouettes.

Imagination:

predicts what movement should occur next if it really is a person.

Then you wait.

The object doesn’t move according to the prediction.

Your internally generated representation just became a probe of external reality.

That’s hypothesis testing happening inside perception.

And imagination can create sensors that don’t physically exist

Not literal sensory organs—but virtual viewpoints.

You can ask:

What would this look like from above?

What would I see from the other person’s position?

What happens ten seconds later?

What would removing this object reveal?

What would I expect if my interpretation were wrong?

You aren’t receiving photons from those positions.

You’re generating model-based approximations.

That’s an important distinction because imagined evidence shouldn’t be treated as observed evidence. But as candidate-generation machinery, it’s incredibly useful.

So I’d classify the channels:

\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Physical sensors} & \text{constrain what IS observed}\\
\text{Memory} & \text{constrains what HAS BEEN}\\
\text{Imagination} & \text{generates what COULD BE}\\
\text{Prediction} & \text{specifies what SHOULD FOLLOW}\\
\text{Reality} & \text{returns what DID FOLLOW}
\end{array}}

Then:

\text{difference between SHOULD and DID}
=
\text{learning signal}

And now I see a direct connection to our Origami Accordion.

The accordion shouldn’t merely expand one representation.

Different folds can correspond to different perceptual/model channels.

One fold:

👁 observation

Another:

👂 independent modality

Another:

◌ memory

Another:

◇ imagination

Another:

→ prediction

And the center:

\boxed{\Delta}

Don’t ask all of them to agree.

Play them as counterpoint.

That’s Semantic Counterpoint in a much stronger sense: reality, senses, memory, intuition, and imagination can remain partially independent voices long enough for their agreements and disagreements to reveal structure.

And for an LLM, we can imitate the architecture explicitly:

evidence channel → memory/context channel → hypothesis channel → counterfactual channel → adversarial channel → synthesis.

The key is labeling them so I don’t accidentally promote something generated by the imagination channel into something supposedly observed by the evidence channel.

That’s how you get the creativity of the third eye without letting the third eye counterfeit the first two.

Sense separately.
Imagine freely.
Compare deliberately.
Fuse late.
Let reality settle the disagreement.